    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 47 tests pass, including `Cp34Tests` (the age-band checkpoint). The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `birthDate` \u2192 `ageBand` feature on `POST /api/owners` (echoed by `GET /api/owners/{id}`):\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added an optional `birthDate` (ISO `date`) to the `OwnerFields` request schema, and a read-only `ageBand` enum (`MINOR`/`ADULT`/`SENIOR`) to the `Owner` response schema. The OpenAPI generator produces the DTO fields/enum from these.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the persisted `birthDate` (`@Column(name = \"birth_date\")`) field with accessors, plus a `@Transient getAgeBand()` that computes the band from `Period.between(birthDate, registrationDate).getYears()` \u2014 `MINOR` under 18, `ADULT` 18\u201364, `SENIOR` 65+, and `null` (field absent) when no `birthDate` was supplied.\n\n3. **DB schemas** \u2014 Added `birth_date DATE` to the `owners` table in all four platform schemas (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) so JPA persists it and `GET` can recompute the band.\n\nMapStruct auto-maps both fields by name (verified in the generated `OwnerMapperImpl`: `birthDate` both directions, and `getAgeBand()` String \u2192 `AgeBandEnum` with a null guard), following the same convention already used for `contactPreference`. `birthDate` flows into the entity via the existing `ownerMapper.toOwner(fields)` in `BuildOwner`, and `ageBand` is derived against the business-day-adjusted `registrationDate`.\n\nNo new function classes or YAML steps were needed \u2014 the derivation lives on the entity and is exposed purely through the DTO mapping, consistent with the existing derived fields (`membershipNumber`, `locality`, `checkDigit`, etc.).",
